`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"When training your own model is justified",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Pretraining a language model from scratch is expensive, so it needs a reason. The Smol Training Playbook, Hugging Face's account of training its SmolLM3 model, names three reasons to pretrain your own model ",e.jsx(s,{id:"smoltrainingplaybook-2025",children:"Ben Allal et al., 2025"}),". In research, a model you train yourself lets you answer a concrete question that an experiment can falsify. In production, you may need a model that meets constraints of domain, deployment, privacy or regulation that no existing model meets. In strategic openness, an open model of your own can fill a specific need that nobody else has met. The playbook also advises first finding out whether an existing model can be prompted or fine-tuned to do the job. Learning is a fourth reason, and it is this course's own: you build the system to understand how it works. This course is the learning case, the case Raia Hadsell's quote on the opening slide makes."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The production reason assumes that no existing model meets your constraints, and you can test that assumption. Before you pretrain from scratch, benchmark suitable existing models under the same constraints. Build a test bed that exercises the capability you need. Fix a metric and the threshold a model must pass. Then score the open models you could use on that test bed. Training your own model does not pay off when an open model already reaches the threshold, or when you have no evaluation that could show whether your own model succeeds."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Once you do train, the same care applies to each decision inside the run. Practitioners run small ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"ablations"}),", experiments that change one setting and measure its effect, before they commit compute to a large run, as the Smol Training Playbook describes ",e.jsx(s,{id:"smoltrainingplaybook-2025",children:"Ben Allal et al., 2025"}),"."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{build:!0,title:"One change reaches across the pipeline",accent:2,number:"11",steps:"5",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{"data-build-step":"1",children:"Tokenizer (text to tokens): sequence length, embedding-table size"}),`
