`,e.jsx(t.li,{"data-build-step":"5",children:"Pills: course week (W), mixture of experts (MoE)"}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"How to trace a dependency through the pipeline",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A design choice in one part of a language-model system changes results in other parts. The tokenizer's segmentation sets how many tokens a text becomes, and so the sequence length. Its vocabulary size is the number of rows in the embedding table. The KV cache that inference keeps for each sequence is explained with connection 4 below. Evaluation choices include the benchmark and the settings at inference time."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The map shows three coupled regions. The model region follows one input from the data through the tokenizer, the embeddings, a stack of ",e.jsx(i,{math:"N",display:!1})," Transformer blocks and the output layer (the LM Head box) to the output tokens. The pipeline region lists the stages in order: data curation, pretraining with its optimization and parallelization, post-training, and inference. The evaluation region stands beside both, because evaluation runs after every stage. Its box names perplexity, the exponential of the mean next-token loss that the Next-Token Prediction tab defines, and downstream benchmarks. Pills on each stage box name the weeks that cover it. The stepper under the map draws ten connections between the boxes, one at a time, each with a caption."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The ten connections come in this order:"}),e.jsxs(t.ol,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Data quality determines what pretraining can learn."}),`
