`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"The decoding strategy, the prompt template and quantization at inference time can change benchmark scores substantially."}),`
`]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Each caption names a dependency to inspect in a real system, and how strongly it holds varies from system to system."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["To see how one dependency crosses region boundaries, follow a change from the configuration through the tensor shapes to an outcome you can measure. Take one change to the batch configuration: reduce ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"micro_batch_size"})," from 4 to 2, and increase ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"gradient_accumulation_steps"})," from 8 to 16. A micro-batch is the set of sequences that one forward and backward pass processes. Gradient accumulation adds up the gradients of several micro-batches before one optimizer update."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["In the model region, the micro-batch size is the leading axis ",e.jsx(i,{math:"B",display:!1})," of two tensors. The logits have shape ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[B,S,V]"}),". At each of the ",e.jsx(i,{math:"S",display:!1})," positions where the model makes a prediction, the logits give the model's scores over the ",e.jsx(i,{math:"V",display:!1})," vocabulary entries. The raw token windows have shape ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[B,S+1]"}),". They hold ",e.jsx(i,{math:"S+1",display:!1})," tokens, one more than the ",e.jsx(i,{math:"S",display:!1})," positions in the logits. The extra token is needed because each predicted position uses the following token as its training target."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["In the training pipeline, the doubled accumulation keeps the global tokens per optimizer update the same. The old setting is ",e.jsx(i,{math:"4 \\times 8",display:!1,trail:":"})," 4 sequences per micro-batch times 8 accumulation steps. The new setting is ",e.jsx(i,{math:"2 \\times 16",display:!1,trail:":"})," 2 sequences per micro-batch times 16 steps. The old and the new setting both give 32 sequences per update. Each sequence has ",e.jsx(i,{math:"S",display:!1})," scored tokens, so every update scores ",e.jsx(i,{math:"32S",display:!1})," tokens. The evaluation region keeps its own fixed batch settings, so it compares the new checkpoint with the old one on the same metric."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A change like this is usually made to save memory. A forward pass stores activations for every sequence in the micro-batch, so halving ",e.jsx(i,{math:"B",display:!1})," lowers that part of peak memory. More and smaller passes may use the GPU less well. This trace follows no single arrow of the map. It follows one configuration change through all three regions. Whether the change saves memory or costs speed is a question for measurement. Compare peak memory, optimizer-step time and tokens per second on the same hardware, with the same fixed sample sequence."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Declare the experiment before you run it",accent:3,figure:e.jsxs(e.Fragment,{children:[e.jsx(t.img,{src:"images/experiment-cycle.svg",alt:"Build then Measure then Learn cycle, with an outer Scale Up loop",className:"w-full light:hidden"}),e.jsx(t.img,{src:"images/experiment-cycle-light.svg",alt:"Build then Measure then Learn cycle, with an outer Scale Up loop",className:"hidden w-full light:block"})]}),figureSide:"right",figureWidth:"1/3",number:"12",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Reasoning alone cannot tell whether a change helps ",e.jsx(s,{id:"smoltrainingplaybook-2025",children:"Ben Allal et al., 2025"})]}),`
